{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now I can see: Construction level = 15! And the Construction XP ring is empty (0%) — level 15 with XP presumably at level start.\n\nSo the bonus \"Construction — 0.5% off building upgrade costs per level — -7%\" with Construction level 15 → expected -7.5%, not -7%!\n\nHmm! Is the display rounded/truncated? 0.5 × 15 = 7.5. The bonus shows \"-7%\". That's a wrong value (should be -7.5%). But wait — the Builder's Workshop screen also shows \"Builder's discount: 7% off\" for the upgrade cost. So the game applies 7% (maybe integer rounded/truncated). Hmm, if the discount is computed as 0.5%*15 = 7.5% but displayed as integer 7 (truncated), the *actual* discount would be 7.5% while the displayed is 7%. But the Builder's Workshop shows the cost: \"Cost: 46,250 coins\" for Inn. Let me check: base cost? The Inn upgrade cost with 7.5% off vs 7% would differ: If base cost is 50,000: 50,000 × 0.925 = 46,250! Yes! 50,000 × 0.925 = 46,250. With 7% off: 50,000 × 0.93 = 46,500. The displayed cost is 46,250 → the actual discount is 7.5%! \n\nSo the Construction bonus is 7.5%, but the profile bonuses section displays \"-7%\" (truncated). That's a wrong_value bug in the *profile bonuses section*! It should show -7.5%. This is a great catch — within the changed area (#4: bonuses from new sources) and verifiable:\n- Construction level 15 (profile tile)\n- Builder's Workshop: \"Cost: 46,250 coins\" (= 50,000 × 0.925) and \"Builder's discount: 7% off\" (also truncated display!)\nHmm, the Builder's Workshop also shows 7% (a display issue there too). Hmm, both display 7%. So maybe the intended display is integer truncation? If so, the bonus list showing -7% is consistent with the app's display convention (integer). Hmm! That weakens it: maybe the design intends \"+X%\" integers.\n\nBut \"0.5% per level × 15 levels = 7.5%\" — displaying \"-7%\" is mathematically wrong (a user computing expected discount would see -7%). And notably, the Guild Hall upgrade cost 69,375: base? Inn 50,000×0.925=46,250 ✓. Guild Hall base 75,000×0.925=69,375 ✓. So actual discount = 7.5% but displayed 7% in both places. That's a UI truncation bug in two places (including bonuses). Hmm, since both show 7%, it's consistent within the app but wrong vs the math.\n\nIs this the *seeded* bug? The change note says \"Profile bonuses section: the listed bonuses now come from a new set of sources.\" Maybe the seeded bug is exactly this: the new sources compute the Construction bonus from level 15 giving 7.5%, but the list displays a truncated/rounded \"-7%\" — i.e., wrong value. Hmm! The precondition didn't state Construction level, but the app shows level 15. The bonus row says \"0.5% off building upgrade costs per level\" → -7.5% expected.\n\nHmm, but maybe the list intentionally truncates all percentages to integers? The other rows: +5% (prestige), +9, +4. No fractional examples. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the *Inn upgrade discount* display again: \"Builder's discount: 7% off\" — if the app truncates, then the app itself shows 7% but applies 7.5%. That's an inconsistency between displayed discount and applied cost. Hmm, but maybe the base cost isn't 50,000. Let me verify the base: The Inn's \"Cost: 46,250 coins\". Is the base cost 50,000? The Builder's Workshop screen didn't show the base. Common values: 50,000 × 0.925 = 46,250. Very likely. Guild Hall: 75,000 × 0.925 = 69,375 ✓. Church would be? If base 100,000 × 0.925 = 92,500. We didn't see Church cost (cut off).\n\nSo indeed the applied discount is 7.5%, while displayed as 7%.\n\nHmm, is it worth reporting the bonuses \"-7%\" as wrong? The expected from the row's own description (0.5%/level) and level 15 is 7.5%. I'd say yes — this is a \"wrong_value\" in the bonuses section. But the developer might say it's intentional rounding. Hmm. The value is displayed as \"-7%\" — the actual effect is 7.5%. I could report it with the evidence: Construction is level 15 (tile), the row says 0.5% per level, so 7.5% expected; displayed -7%. Quote \"-7%\". Element_ref: the Construction bonus row's \"-7%\" text (element [35] in the Bonuses observation). But my latest observe is the Skills grid (Construction tile). The element ref should be from the latest observe. I need to go back to Bonuses and observe.\n\nHmm, but is the difference meaningful? 0.5% × 15 = 7.5%, displayed -7% — a user would notice. Also, the *actual* discount applied (7.5%) differs from the displayed (7%), which is inconsistent. But the Builder's Workshop screen ALSO shows 7% — so if I report only the bonuses section, the dev might say \"the discount is displayed as rounded elsewhere too\". Hmm.\n\nHmm, hold on. Maybe Construction level is 15 and the *actual* discount is 7.5% — but maybe the cost formula uses a *different* base (e.g., base 49,730?). Let me verify the Inn base cost another way: maybe the Builder's Workshop shows the original cost when no discount? Not shown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the discount is rounded DOWN to the nearest integer percent for the *cost*: 50,000 × (1 - 0.07) = 46,500 ≠ 46,250. So no, the cost uses 7.5%.\n\nSo the app displays 7% but charges 7.5%. This is a real inconsistency. But which is \"the defect\"? The display (7% should be 7.5%). The bonus row in the profile is in my area (changed #4). I'll report it: \"Construction bonus shows -7% but Construction level 15 × 0.5% = 7.5% (and the Builder's Workshop applies 46,250 = 50,000 × 92.5%).\" Expected: -7.5%.\n\nHmm, but hold on — is the \"0.5% per level\" itself correct? Yes, shown in the row. And Construction is 15. So -7.5%.\n\nHmm, wait: maybe the bonus value should be based on (Construction level - 1)? 0.5% × 15 = 7.5; if it's level-1=14 → 7%. Hmm! Maybe the intended base is level 15 giving 7.5%. The row says \"+0.5% per level\" → level 15 → 7.5. Hmm, but maybe the game gives discount from level 0 to 15? Unclear.\n\nHmm, the Builder's Workshop applies 7.5% (46,250/50,000). So the real effect is 7.5% → the bonus row should show -7.5%. That's evidence from the app's own cost computation. I'm confident enough.\n\nAlso the Builder's Workshop's \"Builder's discount: 7% off\" would then be wrong too (but that's outside my areas). The row in the bonuses section is directly in the changed area. Let me report the bonuses row.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me double-check the Inn base cost. Maybe the Inn's cost formula is base 50,000 and the discount is 7% → 46,500, but the displayed 46,250 implies 7.5%. Unless the base is 49,731 (46,250/0.93) — unlikely. Or the cost could include another modifier (e.g., the \"Builder's Workshop\" building level discount?). Hmm, the discount line says 7% off. If the actual cost is 46,250, then the base is 49,731.18 — non-round. Unlikely. So 7.5% applied.\n\nHmm, but where does the 7.5% come from if the display says 7%? The true bonus = 0.5 × 15 = 7.5. So the *display* truncates. Since the app's own cost computation uses the true 7.5%, the profile bonus row's \"-7%\" is a wrong value (display rounding). I'll report.\n\nWait, one more consideration: the bonus row might deliberately display a rounded integer (7%) for readability, consistent with the Builder's Workshop. Then the developer would reject. Hmm. But compare the other bonus rows: \"+5% XP\" (exact), \"+9\", \"+4\". No evidence of rounding policy. And a 7.5% bonus shown as 7% is materially wrong.\n\nI'd rather report this as a *consistency* issue: the bonus row says -7% but the actual discount applied is 7.5% (Construction 15). Hmm, the symptom type \"wrong_value\".\n\nLet me get a fresh observation of the Bonuses screen for the element ref, then file.\n\nActually, I realize the construction bonus may be the seeded bug: \"the listed bonuses now come from a new set of sources\" — maybe the new implementation computes the Construction discount from the *Builder's Workshop building tier* rather than Construction level, yielding -7%?? But the row says \"0.5% off per level\" and the source label is \"Builder's Workshop