{"type": "thinking", "text": "The developer couldn't reproduce. Hmm. Maybe because they consider the discount displayed as 7% (rounded) intentional, or my inference is off. Let me re-verify the Construction level and the discount applied before pushing. Actually, maybe the bonus is computed from *Builder's Workshop building tier* rather than Construction level, and 7% is correct. The row's source label \"Builder's Workshop\" suggests the source is the building! If the building tier gives 7%, then -7% is right. Hmm!\n\nWait: the description \"0.5% off building upgrade costs per level\" — \"per level\" of what? If the Builder's Workshop building has levels/tiers (Tier 0/3 now?), 7% doesn't fit. Hmm.\n\nLet me re-verify the Construction level = 15 screenshot: yes it said \"Construction 15\" with an empty ring. And the Builder's Workshop \"Builder's discount: 7% off\".\n\nHmm, 15 × 0.5 = 7.5. Maybe the app uses integer division/floor at each step? The displayed value could be truncated int (7). If the actual applied discount is 7.5% (cost 46,250), then the display in both places truncates. The developer might consider this intended (display integer). But then the bonus value is technically 7.5% and the list shows 7% — the developer said \"could not reproduce\" probably because they see -7% as matching the discount.\n\nSince it's rejected, drop it. Maybe the real intended number: Construction level 14? Let me double-check the screenshot: \"Construction\" tile shows \"15\". Yes, 15. And the ring was empty.\n\nHmm, maybe the discount is 0.5% per level but *capped/truncated* in the game to whole percent. Then the actual applied discount would be 7% → Inn cost should be 46,500, but it's 46,250. Unless the Inn's base cost is different. Let me verify the Inn base cost from another source: maybe the Cost includes other modifiers (e.g., a different building's bonus). Hmm. Without the base, I can't prove 7.5%. The dev rejected. Move on.\n\nAlso note: my calculation of Total Level